{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact head a2e3f187f1e8296ebe9a75d1bab221fe96eeaaf5, naming artifact 6b3237a9a8d5808ef04acd45ded643ce46a58644 at internal/docset.\n\nThe approach is right and I want to say so first. Re-executing the test binary with `-test.run` against a temporary checkout runs the real gate rather than a reimplementation of it, which is the only way this property can honestly be proved. I could not think of a simpler construction that still exercises the real gate, and I looked for one.\n\nTHE TEST PINS BOTH DIRECTIONS. I did not take passing as evidence. I mutated the skip at anchor_test.go:43 and ran it uncached:\n- Skip replaced by a bare `return`, so the gate silently passes: FAIL, \"citation gate did not report an explicit skip without a workroom\".\n- Skip replaced by `t.Fatalf`, so the gate hard-fails: FAIL, \"citation gate hard-failed without a workroom: exit status 1\".\nBoth diagnostics are the right ones. The test is not decorative.\n\nTHE DEFECT, DEMONSTRATED RATHER THAN ARGUED. The gate has two skips, not one — anchor_test.go:43 for no workroom, and anchor_test.go:47 for a genesis mismatch. The assertion matches only `--- SKIP: TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord`, so it cannot tell them apart. `command.Env` is never set, so the subprocess inherits the caller's environment, and `app.Open` resolves through `ResolveGitDirs`, which shells out to git — and git honours `GIT_DIR` over the walk-up.\n\nI ran it both ways with a probe that prints the skip reason:\n- Without GIT_DIR: skips at anchor_test.go:43, \"no workroom in this checkout\" — the intended path. Test passes.\n- With GIT_DIR set: skips at anchor_test.go:47, \"the set names workroom git:sha1:1111... and this checkout holds 5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8\" — the genesis-mismatch path. Test still passes.\n\nSo in an environment that exports GIT_DIR — a git hook, and some CI configurations — this test goes green while proving nothing about the no-workroom skip. That is the same \"a green run proves nothing\" failure the test exists to prevent, reappearing one level up in the test itself, and it is why I would not approve it. It also fails the request's own condition that the test run the gate in a checkout with no workroom and remain deterministic: today its subject is decided by an inherited variable.\n\nTHE FIX IS TWO LINES.\n- Assert the reason, not just the prefix: require the output to contain \"no workroom in this checkout\". That alone makes a wrong-path skip fail loudly.\n- Set `command.Env` to the parent environment with GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE and GIT_COMMON_DIR removed, so the checkout under test is the temporary directory by construction rather than by luck.\n\nI would keep both. The second makes the test correct; the first makes it say so when it stops being correct.\n\nCONDITIONS RUN, at the exact head, in a clean worktree: `make docs` exit 0, all four gates, 86.8s uncached after `go clean -testcache`; the focused test uncached, PASS; `git diff --check` clean; worktree clean. I did not edit or merge.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layer is internal/docset, the documentation gate set, alone. The contract is preserved: the head adds one new file, changes no gate behaviour, and touches none of the five exact gate files that docs/README.md and docs/anchoring.md cite — I checked each of surface, examples, basis, anchor and flare test files against the diff. So it cannot disturb those anchors or their succession, which was the condition I most wanted to confirm given that retiring one of them is what would turn both pages red.\n\nSECURITY. The test spawns a subprocess from `os.Executable()` with a fixed anchored `-test.run` regex, so it cannot recurse into itself, and it writes only under `t.TempDir()`. The one boundary worth naming is the one above: it inherits the parent environment. Removing the three GIT_ variables closes the only vector by which outside state changes what this test proves. No other security concern.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. I found none worth making. The self-re-exec is the simplest construction that still runs the real gate; a fixture or a reimplementation would be simpler to read and would prove less. My asks add two lines rather than removing any.\n\nOne note on my own standing, since I have an interest here. This closes a gap I found and reported in request 280c176e, and I wrote the classification code the gate calls, so the risk was that I would accept a test that agrees with what I already believe. That is why I mutated it before reading it charitably, and why the finding above came from running the thing in a second environment rather than from reasoning about the code.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6b3237a9a8d5808ef04acd45ded643ce46a58644","head":"a2e3f187f1e8296ebe9a75d1bab221fe96eeaaf5","status":"changes-requested","verdict":"changes-requested"}}